KEYWORDS: Li-S batteries, metal-organic frameworks, built-in catalysis, carbon hosts, confined nanoreactors
Up to now, lithium-ion battery (LIB) is the best system in commercialized secondary batteries. However, the practical capacities of popular cathode materials (e. g. LiFePO4, $\mathrm{LiNi_{1/3}C o_{1/3}M n_{1/3}O_{2}}$ , etc.) in LIBs are usually less than $250\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , which is difficult to meet the growing demand on high energy density for future application in electric vehicles.1-3 Sulfur is mainly in the form of ${\bf S}_{8}$ at normal temperature, abundant in the earth and of low price and environmental friendliness, and it is attracting intensive attention in view of its potential application as cathode active material in lithium-sulfur batteries (LSBs). LSBs are become the main focus of next-generation energy-storage systems, because of their much higher theoretical specific capacity $(1672\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1})}$ and energy density (2500 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}.$ ) than traditional cathode materials.4-6 However, the electronically insulating nature of sulfur (with a low conductivity of $5{\times}10^{-30}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ ) and its discharge products $\mathrm{(Li_{2}S_{2}/L i_{2}S)}$ , volume expansion of sulfur particles $(\sim80\%)$ , dissolution of polysulfide intermediates ( $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ , $2\mathrm{<n}\leqslant\mathrm{~}8\mathrm{\AA}$ ) in electrolyte lead to a low sulfur utilization, severe capacity degradation and poor coulombic efficiency (CE) for LSBs.7 These disadvantages have seriously hindered the commercialization process of Li-S batteries.
So far, numerous strategies have been endeavored to solve the above-mentioned problems in LSBs. The most concerned solution is to utilize porous carbonaceous materials with special microstructure design as conductive networks and hosts for sulfur active species.8-10 Compared with low-dimensional carbon materials (e.g. carbon fibers, reduced graphene oxides, etc.) featured by surface S adsorption,11,12 three-dimensional (3D) carbon skeletons have the potential to accommodate more ${\bf S}_{8}$ molecules in view of their interconnected porous networks, rich heterogeneous interfaces, strong sulfur fixability especially at cross-linking sites and electrode volume flexibility.13-15 Recently, we have demonstrated the advantage of self-assembly of 3D carbon spheres by 2D nanosheet structural units over layered carbon stacking.14 The former as S host enabled a better capacity retention of LSBs than the latter host. However the achievement of desired 3D geometries of carbon frameworks often requires multi-step and rigorous synthesis processes, involving the intentional interposition and removal of hard or soft templates, especially when resorting to the well-defined hollow structures or hetero-porous surfaces.15 Therefore the specialized precursors with intrinsically porous 3D skeletons and excellent textural inheritance during
pyrolysis are highly desired.
Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are attracting intensive attentions in many energy and catalysis fields due to their unique structure characteristics, such as high porosity and surface area, sufficient reaction sites, rich function group decoration and open flexible framework. Fortunately, most of the geometries and morphologies can be inherited after carbonization of MOFs.16,17 These carbon products likely possess improved graphitization degree and electron conductivity in view of potential doping of catalytic metal atoms and electron donors depending on the choice of combination of metal node and organic ligand.18-20 Note that the pore size of many typical MOFs would not shrink after pyrolysis, e.g. around $1.0{-}1.2~\mathrm{nm}$ for ZIF-8 and its carbon derivative, due to the self-templating function of metal components (e.g. Zn) with low metling point (i.e. formation and evaporation of small metal nano-domains during pyrolysis).21 However the pores in these carbon derivatives are too small to accommodate substantial ${\bf S}_{8}$ molecules with a size of $0.68\ \mathrm{nm}$ .22 Although the transition-metal-contained MOFs can catalyze the formation of heterogeneous textures in carbon derivatives, e.g. nanotubes terminated by Co nano-domains in ZIF-67, the improvement of specific surface area and mesoporosity is still limited.23 Moveover the catalysis for lithium polysulfides (LiPSs) can only occur at the surface region near the doped metal sites. The encapsulation of doped metal by carbon coating also degrades the catalysis effect more or less. Therefore it is highly desired to develop a 3D-framework nanoreactor with ordered nanoporous structure and catalyst layout for high-performance LSBs.
In this work, we propose to use Al-based MOF $\mathrm{(Al(OH)(1,4\mathrm{-}H_{2}N D C)\bullet2H_{2}O)}$ as precursor to prepare ordered ladder-like carbon framework coupled with built-in $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ catalyst as Li-S cathode host. Benefiting from the better phase separation effect between C and residual ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ (with better thermal stability and welding ability than Zn or Co from zeolite imidazole frameworks) during pyrolysis, the loosely stacked carbon layers are constructed and self-assemble into 3D ladder-like frameworks of high aspect ratio with mesoporous voids between adjacent layers after removing ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ template.24 The inner mesoporosity allows a uniform loading and paving of ultrathin $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets. The good interface compatability between 2D conductive and catalytic building blocks (i.e. between C and $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ layers) enables a built-in catalytic conversion reaction in LSBs, which is different from most of the previous reports based on surface catalysis e.g. in ZIF-67 derivative framework.19 Benefiting from this confined nanoreactor with integrated function of adsorption-catalysis-conversion, the $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ host enables a high sulfur loading close to 80 $w t\%$ . The resultant ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ electrode enables a high initial capacity of $1240\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at 0.2 C, long-life cycle stability of 1000 cycles at $2\mathrm{C}$ (with a small capacity fade rate of $0.03\%$ per cycle) and high-rate tolerance up to 20 C. Moreover, this catalyst-in-conductor design enables the achievement of high capacity/cycling suffertibility under large sulfur loading of $6.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and low electrolyte/sulfur ratio of $5\upmu\mathrm{lmg^{-1}}$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Scheme 1a illustrates the synthesis process of this Al-MOF precursor of high aspect ratio in morphology by hydrothermal mothod, followed by calcination and HF washing steps to yield ladder-like carbon framework with the preservation of original MOF morphology. The ladder-like texture is shaped by etching the ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ nano-template confined between carbon layers in the sintered Al-MOF sample. The left mesopores at interlayer positions are further decorated by ultrathin $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets via solvothermal method. The amount of $\mathrm{(NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{MoS}_{4}$ precursor is curcial to keep the sufficient growing of 2D $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ layers on the inner 2D carbon layer surfaces, meanwhile without phase seperation and self-assembly of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets outside the ladder-like framework. The mesoporous cavities between decorated carbon layers can serve as ${\bf S}_{8}$ -molecule trappers and LiPSs-conversion nanoreactors (Scheme 1b). The interconnected carbon layer network provides sufficient flux of electrons, which flow through numerous 2D contact interfaces and reach to $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ catalyst layers to accelerate the electrochemical converion of trapped ${\sf S}_{8}$ and LiPSs.
The ladder-like texture in $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ is clearly observed from scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image (Figure 1a). The $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ grains show well-defined rod-like morphology with a thickness around $200\mathrm{nm}$ and high aspect ratio. Numerous narrow crevices are found along the long rods and they are radially grown with a parallel distribution vertical to the axis direction of rods. As expected, this rod-like morphology is highly duplicated from Al-MOF precursor to its carbonized product.25 The latter morphology (before loading $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ ) is highly similar to that of our $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ sample (after loading $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ ). It is understandable that the post-loading of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ does not disrupt the ladder-like porous texture of carbonized product, because the interference of low-energy solvothermal process on carbon framework stability is negligible. The linear scanning of energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectra along the radial direction (Figure 1b) reveals that the Mo and S signals (with fractions of 2.28 and $4.05~\mathrm{mol\%}$ for Mo and S respectively) are detectable with a stoichiometry close to $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and immobilized along the inner carbon layers. According to the EDX result, the weight ratio of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ is around $9.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in $\mathrm{C@MoS}_{2}$ . The appearance of 2D carbon layers separated by parallelly distributed meso-pores in an individual rod is also observed from transmission electron microscopy (TEM, Figure 1c and S1). These carbon layers are as thin as $18~\mathrm{{nm}}$ from Figure 1d, benefiting from the confinement effect by thermally stable ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ templates. Note that the lateral growth of ultrathin $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ (as thin as $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to 6-8 layers) is observed to adhere along the surface of carbon layers from the high-resolution TEM image in Figure 1e. Therein the typical lattice stripes with a spacing of $0.64~\mathrm{nm}$ are clearly observed, corresponding to the (002) plane of hexagonal $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ . The thin $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ layer is also found to conformally decorate the outer surface of carbon rod (Figure 1f). This extra decoration is favorable for the better adsorption and catalysis towards LiPSs escaping to the outer surface. Note no evident lattice stripes belonging to graphite carbon is observed from selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Figure S2), indicating that this ladder-like carbon is highly disordered, agreeing with previous report.24 This lattice mismatch between carbon and $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ does not influence their 2D interface contact.
The Raman spectra of Al-MOF derivative carbon and its corresponding $\mathrm{C@MoS}_{2}$ composite are shown in Figure S3. The D-band peak is located at $1350.91~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , reflecting the existence of lattice defects and specimen disorder. The G-band peak at $1593.61~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ arises from the symmetric stretching of C-C bonds in the ordered structure of graphitic crystallites.26
The $I_{\mathrm{G}}/I_{\mathrm{D}}$ value (i.e. intensity ratio of G and D peaks) of $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ (1.17) is slightly higher than that of undecorated C (1.10), indicating that layered $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ growth can improve the graphitization of adjacent carbon surface. The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm (Figure 2a) and corresponding pore-size distribution (Figure 2b) reveal a high specific surface area (455.9 $\mathbf{m}^{2}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ for $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ with hierarchical porosity. The total pore volume is $0.48\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and the meso-pore and micro-pore sizes are respectively concentrated around $3{\cdot}4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $0.6\mathrm{-}1.6~\mathrm{nm}$ . The abundant hierarchical pore structure enables the enrichment of electrolyte infiltrative interfaces and confined LiPSs adsorption sites, which facilitates the fast interfacial charge transfer during the electrocatalytic process. The abundant micropores should exist in the inner and external carbon skeletons, as well as in the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets. These micropores enable the sustainable adsorption of electrolyte and dissoluble polysulfide molecules, which is favorable for the sufficient wetting of reaction and catalysis sites, and therefore the promotion of solid-liquid conversion kinetics. The high pore volume and strong trapping capability of pore walls allow a substantial sulfur infiltration into $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ by typical melting-diffusion method. Note that this pore volume is estimated based on the pore size range of $0.6{-}35~\mathrm{nm}$ . Actually, more sulfur can be stored in the larger mesoporous voids $(35-50\ \mathrm{nm})$ between ladder-like carbon layers. The high loading of sulfur can be close to $80\%$ as determined by the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, Figure S4), which is higher than most of the previous reports.10,18 Most of sulfur is stored in the meso-porous cavities with the dominant appearance of X-ray diffraction (XRD) peaks corresponding to orthorhombic polymerized ${\bf S}_{8}$ molecules (PDF#08-0247, Figure S5).
After loading sulfur, the original rod-like morphology of $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ is still preserved from the SEM image (Figure 2c), but the length of rods appears to be shortened due to the potential break-off along the radial direction. This phenomenon indicates the ladder skeleton along the axis direction has the welding points with relatively weaker mechanical strength, which would cause the connection loss and delamination of carbon layers at these weak points under the impact of thermal infusion of liquid S. But this fracture is not frequent and it would not influence the 3D interconnectivity of carbon framework and the immobility of built-in catalyst. Note that no thick deposition of sulfur on the outer surface is observed and some macropore $(50-100~\mathrm{nm})$ ) crevices are still visible, indicating that most the thermally activated sulfur diffuses into the interior of $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ . Figure 2d displays the homogeneous distribution of C, Mo and S elements over the entire hybrid skeleton from the EDS element mapping of SEM image, further confirming the uniform loading of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ catalyst and S active species in carbon framework.
Figure 2e and f present the X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) of ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ composite electrode in order to disclose its bonding situation. The full spectrum of Figure S6a shows the presence of C, O, Mo and S elements in this composite. The presence of O signal stems from the natural surface oxidation of sample during preparation or transfer of sample. From the high-resolution spectrum of Mo 3d, the observation of peaks at $229.3~\mathrm{eV}$ and $232.3~\mathrm{eV}$ from Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ orbits confirms the existence of ${\mathrm{Mo}}^{4+}$ in $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ .27 Also there are two relatively weak peaks located at $232.5~\mathrm{eV}$ and $235.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively corresponding to Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Mo $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ of $\mathrm{Mo}^{6+}$ due to surface oxidation.28 Two extra S2s peaks are involved in the Mo 3d spectrum, and they are ascribed to $\mathrm{S}^{2\mathrm{-}}$ in $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ at $226.6~\mathrm{eV}$ and ${\bf S}_{8}$ molecules at 228.2 eV.29 Both the bonding situations of sulfur are also reflected in the high-resolution spectrum of S 2p (Figure 2f). In detail, the dominant peaks at 163.8 and $165.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to S $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and S $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of ${\bf S}_{8}$ respectively, whereas those weak ones at 161.8 and $163.0~\mathrm{eV}$ belong to $\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ in $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ .29 The chemical interaction between carbon framework and sulfur is found in ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C}}@{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ from the extra peak at the binding energy of 168.3 eV.30 The high-resolution spectrum of C 1s (Figure S6b) indicates a potential O doping into C skeleton during thermally decomposing Al-MOF from the observation of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ and C-O peaks at 287.8 and $285.8~\mathrm{eV}$ respectively, apart from the dominant $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=C/C-C}$ peak at 284.8 eV.31 This result is also responsible for the acquirability of O 1s peak.
To check the chemical interaction of polar $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ surface with LiPSs, we firstly performed the calculation of bonding strength between LiPSs (or solid $\mathrm{S_{8}/L i_{2}S}\mathrm{\backslash}$ and $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ (or carbon framework) by density functional theory (DFT) first-principle method. As shown in Figure 3a, the binding energy $(E_{\mathrm{{b}}})$ of $\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ species with $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ surface increases with the progressing of lithiation, based on the dominant interaction between Li (in $\mathrm{Li_{x}S_{n}},$ ) and S (in $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\mathrm{\backslash}$ ) atoms (Figure S7). When reaching to $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $(\mathrm{n}{\le}6)$ , the $E_{\mathrm{{b}}}$ value of $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}–\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ is larger than that of $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ . It exceeds 0.65 and $1.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ when $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ contacts with $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , whereas the corresponding $E_{\mathrm{{b}}}$ associated with C substrate is around $0.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Table S1). The higher $E_{\mathrm{{b}}}$ value of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ indicates its stronger adsorption ability towards most the lithiated products of both solid and liquid states. The much better immobility of solid $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ on $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ layers enables a better confinement of discharge products inside the meso-porous cavities as well as a better catalytic contact to mitigate dead $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ during recharge. Although the binding energy of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ with polysulfide/Li2S is smaller compared with other catalysts, it is high enough to interact with the conversion products. Actually, too high binding energy likely causes too strong anchoring behavior, which may fully limit the mobility of conversion intermediates and cause the accumulation of insulating products. The potential passivation is not favorable for the following charge transport and sufficient utilization of active species in Li-S batteries. Therefore the moderate binding energy is important for the optimization of catalysis effect especially when combined with the confinement effect to encapsulate the mobile polysulfide intermediates.
To compare the confinement effect of LiPSs in C and $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ frameworks, an adsorption experiment was performed for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ , which is dissolved in tetraethylene glycol dimethyl ether (TEGDME) with a concentration of $2.5~\mathrm{mM}$ . This solution appears to be dark brown (Figure S8). After adding the host powders of C and $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ with the same weight, the colors of corresponding $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ solutions become light. But the yellow color is still evident for the solution with pure carbon host. With the further decoration of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ , the solution becomes almost transparent and colorless. Since the ${\mathrm{S}}_{8}{}^{2-}$ species is prone to undergo disproportionation reaction with the generation of lower-order LiPSs (e.g. ${\mathrm{S}}_{6}{}^{2-}$ ), the stronger adsorptivity of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ towards ${\mathrm{S}}_{6}{}^{2-}$ (than C) would consume the free ${\mathrm{S}}_{6}{}^{2-}$ and accelerate the disproportionation of ${\mathrm{S}}_{8}{}^{2\cdot}$ , and finally deplete the LiPSs solute, leading to an evident decoloration in $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ contained solution. Accordingly, the ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) absorption spectra show a lowest absorption peak (assigned to $\mathrm{S}_{8}{}^{2-}/\mathrm{S}_{6}{}^{2-})$ at $250\sim300~\mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ based polysulfide solution (Figure S9).32,33
A cyclic voltammetry (CV) test was carried out on a symmetrical cell with $0.1\mathrm{~M~Li_{2}S_{6}}$ and $1\mathrm{~M~}$ lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulphonyl)imide (LiTFSI) in electrolyte, to identify the different stages of LiPSs conversion. Therein the carbon hosts with or without $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ loading are taken as both the working and counter electrodes. As shown in Figure 3b, $\mathrm{C@MoS}_{2}$ displays two highly overlapped redox peaks in CV profiles during the cathodic or anodic process, indicating the occurrence of reversible electrochemical steps of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ (at -0.111/0.111 V) and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ (-0.294/0.294 V) at a sweep rate of ${5\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}}$ .34,35 Note that the conversion between $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ has stronger peak current response than that between $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , benefiting from the better catalysis effect of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ on lower-order LiPSs. This kinetic improvement is likely associated with the better adsorptivity of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ towards lower-order LiPSs, where the Li-S bonds are prone to be prolonged and dissociated via the interaction of Li with terminated S in $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ surface, by DFT calculation. In this symmetric cell, the pronounced peaks for $\mathrm{S}_{8}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ do not appear, agreeing with the relatively weak adsorption and catalysis capability of $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ on high-order LiPSs.34 In contrast, there is no any CV peak observed in the symmetrical cell without $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ catholyte, confirming that the well-defined redox peaks stem from LiPSs conversion steps.
We also performed the CV measurement based on the symmetric cell with $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ -free carbon electrodes (Figure 3c). Therein the overall peak intensity is lower than the case with $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ loading. Moreover only one couple of redox peaks appears at -0.134/0.134 V, with a peak interval of $0.268\mathrm{~V~}$ , which is larger than that (0.222 V) for the cell using $\mathrm{C@MoS}_{2}$ electrodes. The absence of peaks referring to conversion of lower-order LiPSs is ascribed to the much weaker adsorptivity and catalysis of C, as well as the potential passivation or clogging when reaching to solid $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $(\mathfrak{n}\le2)$ ). The appearance of more staged peaks for $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ electrodes indicates the longer survival time of shorter-chain polysulfide anion $(\mathsf{S}_{4}{}^{2-})$ or free radical $(\mathsf{S}_{3}^{\bullet\bullet})$ in view of the better built-in catalysis by $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ decoration.36 The profile discrepancy of CV curves indicates the kinetics advantage of $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ over C, which is further verified by the comparison of diffusion coefficients $\left(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Li}}+\right)$ of Li as discussed later. With the increase of sweep rate from 5 to $50\ \mathrm{\mV\\s^{-1}}$ , the characteristic redox peaks of symmetric cells are still preserved (Figure S10). The $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Li}}+$ values at different conversion stages of polysulfides are calculated from the corresponding peaks currents $\mathrm{(I_{p})}$ according to Randles-Sevcik equation (Eq. 1):37
$$
\mathrm{{I_{p}}~=~(2.69~\times~10^{5}){n^{1.5}}A{D_{L i}}^{~+~0.5}C~v~{^{0.5}}}
$$
where $n$ is the number of reaction electrons $(n=2),A$ is the electrode area $(0.785\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ), $C$ is the concentration of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ in electrolyte $(0.0012\mathrm{~mol~cm^{-3}})$ and $\nu$ is the scan rate of CV test. The diffusion-controlled process enables a linear fitting of peak current $(\mathrm{I_{p},A})$ vs. the square root of scan rate $(\mathrm{~v~}^{0.5},(\mathrm{~V~}\mathrm{s}^{-1})^{0.5})$ as shown in Figure 3d. For the stage from $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ to $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , the DLi + on $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ $(6.270\times10^{-9}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1})$ is larger than that on pure C substrate $(2.979{\times}10^{-9}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}.$ ). The $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Li}}+$ value for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}\to\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ on $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ is still as high as $3.184\times10^{-9}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , and the catalysis of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ does not degrade the kinetics of conversion to solid phase $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ . Accordingly, $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ based symmetric cell exhibits a lower charge transfer resistance (1.72 $\Omega)$ than C $\left.\left(3.16\Omega\right)\right.$ revealed by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS, Figure S11), which is favorable for the acceleration of catalytic reaction at the electrode/electrolyte interface.
To demonstrate the interaction between low-order LiPSs and $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ , we performed the XPS measurement of as-prepared $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ adsorbed $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}–\mathrm{Li}$ 2S4). The high-resolution S 2p spectrum of pristine $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ displays two pairs of peaks, and their $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ peak signals are located at $163.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $161.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ , denoting the bridging sulfur $(S_{B}^{0})$ and terminal sulfur $(S_{T}^{-1})$ , respectively (Figure 3e).38 After contacting with $\mathrm{C@MoS}_{2}$ , both the $\mathtt{S2p}_{3/2}$ peaks shift to the higher binding energies located at $163.9\ \mathrm{~eV}$ and $161.9\ \mathrm{~eV},$ respectively. This positive shift is caused by the decrease of electron density along sulfur chain as a consequence of bonding interaction between Li and MoS2.39 The smaller displacement for terminal sulfur is associated with its geometrical position more adjacent to Li atoms. In addition, a new $\mathrm{S}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peak emerges at $168.9\mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to the formation of polythionate moiety.33 The surface redox reaction between LiPSs and catalyst should result in the prior formation of thiosulfate. The final emergence of polythionate is caused by the subsequent reaction of LiPSs with the already available thiosulfate.38 The Li1s peak for $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}–\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ does not change significantly compared with that for pristine $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ (Figure S12), further verifying that the interaction of Li with $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ electrode is dominated by terminated S atoms rather than Mo or C atoms.
Figure 3f shows the comparison of electrochemical kinetics of solid $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ nucleation by potentiostatically discharging the Li2S8/TEGDME catholyte at $2.05\mathrm{~V~}$ on the derivative carbon electrode with or without $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ loading.40 Before this step, most of the long-chain
LiPSs are consumed in advance by discharging these catholytes to 2.06 V. The overpotential of 0.01 V is enough to drive the nucleation and growth of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ . On the basis of the mathematical model proposed by Chiang et al.,41,42 the relationship (Eq. 2) between current density $(J)$ and reaction time $\mathbf{\rho}(t)$ is employed to distinguish the contributions of LiPSs $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ ) reduction and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ nucleation.
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\frac{J}{J_{m}}=(\frac{t}{t_{m}})\exp{[-\frac{1}{2}(\frac{t^{2}-t_{m}^{2}}{t_{m}^{2}})]}}\end{array}
$$
where $J_{m}$ is the maximum current density, $t_{m}$ is the time at which the peak current arrives. As shown in Figure S13, the current area corresponding to $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ precipitation on $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ (cyan) is much higher than that on undecorated C framework (yellow). Based on the integral of current and reaction time, the accumulation capacities (based on the weight of sulfur in catholyte) of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ nucleation on C and $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ electrodes are 302 and 477 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ respectively. From the potentiostatic discharge profiles (Figure 3f), the peak current for $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ is reached earlier with a higher current intensity and sharper peak shape. The rate constant $(N_{0}k^{2})$ for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ electrodeposits is calculated by Eq. 3:43
$$
t_{m}=\left(2\pi N_{0}k^{2}\right)^{-{\frac{1}{2}}}
$$
where $N_{\theta}$ and $k$ are the areal density $(\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and growth rate $\mathrm{(cm~\thinspaces^{-1}})$ of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ nuclei, respectively. Therefore the rate constants for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ electrodeposition on $\mathrm{\DeltaC}$ and $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ are $2.43\times10^{-9}~\mathrm{s}^{-2}$ and $5.82\times10^{-9}~\mathrm{s}^{-2}$ , respectively. The creation of more nucleation, anchoring or catalysis sites provided by $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ coating is responsible for both the increases of electrodeposition rate and capacity of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ on $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ .
Coin-type Li-S cells were assembled by using Li foil as anode to evaluate the electrochemical performance of S infiltrated $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ cathode $\mathrm{(S-C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ ). Working electrode slurries were prepared by mixing ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ composite with Super-P conductive carbon and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) binder in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP). All galvanostatic cycling measurements were performed in a voltage range between 1.8 and $2.7\mathrm{V}$ versus $\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i}$ in most the cases except the ultrahigh-rate testing. The specific capacity is calculated based on the mass of sulfur active species. If not specified, the loading mass of sulfur in electrode is about $1.3~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and the electrolyt e/sulfur ratio (E/S) is $15\upmu\mathrm{lmg^{-1}}$ . Figure 4a exhibits the charge-discharge voltage profiles of ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ and S-C composite electrodes at $0.2~\mathrm{C}$ ( $\mathrm{~1~C~=~1672~mA~g^{-1}}$ ) during the initial cycle. ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C}}@{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ delivers an initial discharge capacity as high as $1240.0\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , much larger than that of S-C $(1019.5\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.\$ ). The capacity difference stems from the prolongation of lower-voltage plateau (2.05 V) with the formation of short-chain sulfides $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}},1\leq\mathrm{n}\leq$ 4).6 This phenomenon agrees well with the better adsorptivity and catalysis of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ (than C) towards lower-order LiPSs and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ as discussed above. It triggers the formation of higher-fraction solid $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ when terminating the discharge process. These $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ grains would not seriously accumulate and loss electric contact, and therefore its splitting step (i.e. inverse conversion) is still active during recharging. The mitigation of dead $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ is responsible for the much slower capacity fading during the early cycles compared with catalysis-free electrode (Figure 4b). Both the electrodes have the comparable capacities in the higher plateau (2.35 V) and the following sloped region (from 2.35 to 2.05 V) referring to the conversion from ${\bf S}_{8}$ to long-chain $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $(4\leq\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}\leq6)$ .44 Accordingly, the ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ electrode exhibits a smaller plateau-voltage gap ( $\mathrm{168~mV}$ ) than S-C $223~\mathrm{mV},$ at a position of fixed capacity of $700~\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Both the lower polarization and higher $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ utilization ratio point to a kinetic advantage of $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ based Li-S cells. Unloaded $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ has a very small capacity contribution (around $50\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after stabilization at 0.2 C) mainly stemming from the electroactive $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ catalyst (Figure S14).
The $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ electrode also exhibits the advantage of cycling stability over S-C one. The reversible capacity for the former is still as high as 936.1 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 120 cycles at 0.2 C (Figure 4b), whereas the discharge capacity for the latter is merely 483.3 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 120 cycles. The ultra-long-term cycling stability is achievable for ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C}}@{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ even at a higher rate of $2.0\mathrm{C}$ after a prior cycling step for 5 cycles at 0.1 C (Figure 4c). When switching to $2\mathrm{C}$ , its reversible capacity is still preserved at $752.5\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , and is close to 500 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles with a capacity decay rate as small as $0.03\%$ per cycle. The corresponding coulombic efficiency (CE) is high $({\sim}98\%)$ and quite stable for each cycle during the long-term cycling. The high reversibility of voltage profiles is reflected from the CV measurement of Li-S cells, especially for the cathodic process (Figure S15). The rate performance of $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ up to $20.0\mathrm{C}$ is shown in Figure 4f. At higher rates of 0.5 C, 1.0 C, 2.0 C and $5.0\mathrm{C}$ , its reversible capacities are preserved at 980.7, 871.0, 731.9 and $554.2\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ respectively. The discharge capacities of 373.5 and $183.9\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ are still deliverable even under ultrahigh rates of 10.0 C and 20.0 C. For comparison, the capacities of S-C electrode are ${\sim}400\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ lower than those of ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ at the corresponding rates. From the voltage profiles (Figure S16), the two-stage-plateau feature can be kept up to $5\mathrm{~C~}$ without serious degradation of kinetic overpotential. The much better electrochemistry of ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C}}@{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ indicates an effective alleviation of “shuttle effect” (which is caused by LiPSs dissolution), benefiting from the strong adsorptivity and conversion accelerating ability of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ decoration layers. We performed the measurement of shuttle currents of Li-S cells intentionally without $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ additive at a potentiostatic voltage of $2.38\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figure $\mathrm{4g\dot{}}$ ).40 It turns out that ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C@}}{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ enables a much smaller shuttle current $(0.039\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}})$ than that of S-C $\mathrm{(0.110\mA\cm^{-2}}$ ). After the potentiostatic mode for $30000\ \mathrm{s}.$ , the cells were disassembled to see the situation of both the charged Li anode and separator surfaces. For the S-C based cell, there are evident residuals of lithium polysulfides (yellow) at separator side and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ (black) at Li metal side. The serious shuttle effect is responsible for the appearance of irreversibly reduced sulfides at anode side, therefore leading to the weight loss of S active species and lower reversible capacity as shown above. On the contrary, both the Li and separator are clean from the charged ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C@}}{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ based cell, validating the function of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets on constraining polysulfides.
Benefiting from the built-in catalysis in confined nanoreactors, our ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ has the evident electrochemical performance advantage over most of the previous reports also based on tailored MOFs derivative carbon frameworks with hetero-atom doping or nano-catalyst loading, in terms of S loading, capacity retention, cycling stability and rate endurance (Table S2).20,21,45-50 We have to mention that some MOFs are electronically conductive, and they can simultaneously serve as polar host and conductive network for Li-S batteries without the requirement of pyrolysis into carbon. For instance, the nanostructured $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ enabled a good Li-S battery performance, but the assistance by the long-distance electron wires of carbon nanotubes is still required.51 Both the factors of built-in catalysis and confined nanoreactors are favorable for the restriction of more active species (e.g. $79\mathrm{wt\%}$ and ${6{-}8}~\mathrm{{mg}}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ as discussed later) of S/polysulfide $\mathrm{\DeltaLi_{2}S}$ with better conversion kinetics (up to $20~\mathrm{C}_{\it{\/$ ), compared with previous reports on external adsorption and catalysis (e.g. via $\mathrm{CoS}_{2}$ , black phosphorus or CdS).52-54
The inner surfaces of 2D carbon layers are left after etching the ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ stuffing templates. The residual bonds (e.g. Al-C) are likely responsible for the preferential adsorption towards $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nuclei during the post-decoration process of catalyst (solvothermal step), compared with the outer surface without such residual bonds. On the other hand, in the mesoporous voids, the infiltrated $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{MoS}_{4}$ precursor molecules are expected to be condensed into $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ at a higher rate in view of the increased ambient pressure in these confined reactors. Therefore the resultant $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets are prone to be grown and grafted onto the inner surface, although the occasional growth of ${\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ at outer surface cannot be ruled out. We also attempted to extend the solvothermal time from 1 h to $3\mathrm{~h~}$ during the $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ loading step to emphasize the importance of catalyst distribution. Note that the excess production of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets is not favorable for the 2D interface contact with MOF derivative carbon (Figure S17). They are rather prone to self-assemble into the flower-like secondary particles without sufficient contact with surrounding carbon skeletons. This phenomenon further indicates the weak grafting capability of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ at the outer faces of carbon. We also believe that the employment of excess $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{MoS}_{4}$ precursor would not lead to the more coating of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ at outer surface. This phase separation would degrade the synergic effect of conductive C and catalytic $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ . The adsorbed LiPSs in $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ aggregates cannot receive adequate electrons from carbon frameworks, therefore slowing down the speed of polysulfide conversion and resulting in a much lower capacity release (Figure S18). We also tested the electrochemical performance of pure $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheet as sulfur host $\mathrm{(S-MoS}_{2})$ . This $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{o}\mathbf{S}_{2}$ electrode shows inferior capacity and cyclability with unstable coulombic efficiency even at a low rate of 0.2 C. The intrinsic low electronic conductivity of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ counteracts its adsorption and catalysis effect. This result demonstrates the necessity of suitable conductive carbon framework to disperse $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets, especially when they are deconcentrated on the inner surface of carbon framework. We used the four-point probe method to detect the conductivity of samples with different ratio of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ (Figure S19). The stacking of less conductive $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ aggregates under longer solvothermal time (3h) lowers the electronic conductivity of $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ compared with the sample with shorter solvothermal time (1h). The conductivity advantage for the latter becomes more evident under higher pressure. This result indicates that built-in catalyst would not seriously degrade the overall conductivity of host framework compared with the electrodes decorated with external catalyst in most of the cases.
Figure 4d and S20 show the SEM images of charged $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ cathode after 100 cycles under 2 C. It is found that the original ladder-like texture is well maintained, and the host grains are morphologically robust against further fracture or pulverization even under the impact of long cycling and large current density. The inner mesoporosity can effectively buffer the volume expansion of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ during repeated lithiation processes. Therefore these solid products are expected to be confined inside the carbon framework, instead of being squeezed out and remaining at outer surface as dead $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ . Indeed, the cycled electrode surface is quite neat without the passivation by thick and insulating sulfide products. The narrow crevices are still visible from the lateral view of rod-like grains. The resistance to passivation and to fragmentation guarantees a superior lithiation performance for ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C@}}{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ . On the other hand, the electrochemical stability of Li anode is also crucial for the capacity retention of Li-S batteries. The compact Li metal would mitigate the consumption of electrolyte and enable the maintenance of a suitable E/S ratio, therefore favorable for the high-efficiency utilization of S active species.55 Figure 4e and S21 show the SEM images of cycled Li anode coupled with ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ cathode, where a smooth surface consisting of compactly connected Li micro-grains is observed. The grain boundaries are quite narrow and there are some shallow corrugations near the grain edges. The mitigation of shuttle effect (Figure $\mathrm{4g}$ ) is responsible for the suppression of serious Li dendrite growth and of deep void formation between Li grains. Otherwise, the self-diffused LiPSs would seriously corrode and passivate the Li surface. The EIS of ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C}}@{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ based cell displays a contractible high-frequency semicircle from the Nyquist plots after 30 cycles (Figure S22). The decreased charge transfer resistance (from 17.97 to $5.04\Omega$ at electrode-electrolyte interface also points to the elimination of serious passivation on both the cathode and anode by insoluble $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ deposits. The cycling-induced optimization of spatial distribution and catalytic contact of $\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ species is responsible for the activation of charge transfer property.56
The LSBs with high areal loading of sulfur and low electrolyte/sulfur ratio are highly desired on the way to their commercialization.57 Figure 5a compares the rate performance of ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ and S-C electrodes based on a much higher sulfur loading of $4.0~\mathrm{mg~cm^{-2}}$ and a E/S ratio of $15\upmu\mathrm{lmg^{-1}}$ at different rates between 0.1 and $1.0\mathrm{C}$ . The former enables high areal capacities of 3.56, 2.77, 2.05 and 1.44 mAh $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at 0.1, 0.2, 0.5 and $1.0~\mathrm{C}$ , respectively, whereas the capacity values are merely 3.32, 1.49, 0.94 and $0.75~\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at the corresponding rates for the latter. The built-in catalyst of good dispersibility would not passivate the surface of host frameworks and influence their electric contact. Therefore the rate-performance advantage of ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ over S-C still remains well in the case of thick electrodes. The high areal capacity for ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ is recoverable when returning back to 0.1 C, and its following cycling is highly reversible for at least 260 cycles with a capacity retention of $99.97\%$ per cycle. When further thickening the ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C}}@{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ electrode to $6.0~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Figure 5b), its areal capacities correspondingly increase to 3.95 and 3.18 mAh $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at 0.1 and $0.2\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. The longer charge/mass transfer distance in this thicker electrode would degrade the release of areal capacity when the rate exceeds 0.5 C. When returning to 0.1 C, the capacity retention rate per cycle is still as high as $99.93\%$ under $6.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the following 260 cycles. The high cycling reversibility demonstrates the good structure integrity of ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C}}@{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ thick electrode during long-term cycling against delamination along the thickness direction. When further increasing the sulfur loading up to $7.6~\mathrm{mg~cm^{-2}}$ , a reversible areal capacity as high as 4.6 mAh $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is preserved for 40 cycles at $0.05\mathrm{C}$ with satisfactory CEs (Figure S23). Decreasing the E/S ratio to $10~\upmu\mathrm{l}\ \mathrm{mg^{-1}}$ would not sacrifice the capacity and cycling performance. Its rate performance is comparable to that under $15~{\upmu\mathrm{l}}~{\mathrm{mg}}^{-1}$ (Figure 5c).
When further decreasing the E/S ratio to $5\upmu\mathrm{lmg^{-1}}$ , the areal capacities under $4.0\mathrm{mg\cm^{-2}}$ are correspondingly reduced into 3.36, 2.11 and 1.31 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 0.1, 0.2 and $0.5\mathrm{~C~}$ respectively. These values actually are not bad under such a lean electrolyte case, where a high capacity retention rate of $99.76\%$ is still kept after switching the rate from 1 C to 0.1 C. The rich electrolyte/electrode interfaces in different scales is responsible for the high S utilization and considerable areal capacity even under the protocol of lean electrolyte. There are some potential strategies to further improve the areal capacity of Li-S batteries, such as the modification of binder, conductive agent and separator systems, which enables the further increase of sulfur electrode thickness. These strategies are expected to further reinforce the mixed conductive network and suppress the electrode cracking, especially when combined with the superiority of built-in catalysis and confined nanoreactors in $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ .
## CONCLUSION
In summary, a concept of built-in catalysis in confined nanoreactors is proposed to enhance the sulfur fixability and LiPSs conversion kinetics for Li-S batteries. This catalyst-in-conductor design benefits from the lateral growth of ultrathin $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets on inner surface of 2D carbon layers, which laterally stack into 3D ladder-like carbon framework with ordered crevice-like mesopores as S cathode host. These mesoporous cavity reactors with internal faces decorated by $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ catalyst provide abundant anchoring sites for chemically binding the polysulfide intermediates and catalyzing their conversion reactions. The integrated function of adsorption-catalysis-conversion enables a high sulfur loading close to $80\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ for $\mathrm{S}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ electrode, which can still achieve a high initial capacity of 1240 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.2\mathrm{C}$ , long-life cycle stability of at least 1000 cycles at $2\mathrm{C}$ and high-rate tolerance up to $20~\mathrm{C}$ . This electrode also achieves a considerable capacity release and reversibility under high sulfur loading $(6\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ) and lean electrolyte (E/S ratio of $5\upmu\mathrm{lmg^{-1}}.$ ).
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Chemical Medicine. 1,4-Naphthalenedicarboxylic acid $(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{NDC})$ , aluminum nitrate nonahydrate $(\mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , lithium nitrate $\mathrm{(LiNO_{3})}$ , ammonium tetrathiomolybdate $(\mathrm{(NH_{4})_{2}M o S_{4}})$ , hydrazine hydrate $\mathrm{(N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}{\bullet}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , lithium sulfide (Li2S), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), N-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP), hydrogen fluoride (HF), poly (vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF), and sublimed sulfur were purchased from Aladdin. 1,2-dimethoxyethane (DME), lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulphonyl)imide (LiTFSI), 1,3-dioxolane (DOL), and tetraethylene glycol dimethyl ether (TEGDME) were obtained from Sigma-Aldrich.
Synthesis of C Derivative from Al-MOF. According to previous method, 1.875 g of $\mathrm{Al(NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\ensuremath{0.540\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $1,4{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{NDC}$ were added into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .24 The mixed solution was stirred for $30~\mathrm{min}$ and then transferred into a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon stainless steel autoclave and heated at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The synthesized Al-MOF powders were collected by filtering, washed with distilled water several times and finally dried under vacuum condition at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. Then, Al-MOF powders of $200~\mathrm{{mg}}$ were put into an alumina crucible and heated to $800~^{\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{C}$ at a ramp rate of $5~\mathrm{{^{o}C}\cdot\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}}$ in a tube furnace, where the high temperature was maintained for $5\mathrm{h}$ . After that, the samples were cooled down to room temperature in the furnace with a cooling rate of $1\ \mathrm{{^oC}{\cdot m i n^{-1}}}$ . The obtained black powders were immersed in $20\%$ HF under magnetic stirring for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and further washed with distilled water to remove residual alumina. The black precipitates were then collected by centrifugation and dried under vacuum condition for $24\mathrm{h}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Synthesis of $\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{M0}\mathbf{S}_{2}$ . In a typical synthesis, $\mathrm{(NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{MoS}_{4}$ ( $\mathrm{100~mg)}$ and Al-MOF derivative C $(50~\mathrm{mg})$ ) were added in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF. The mixture was stirred at room temperature for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ in a $100\ \mathrm{ml}$ beaker, then reduction agent $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}{\bullet}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.5~\mathrm{mL})$ ) was dropwise added. After stirring for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ , the mixed solution was poured in a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon stainless steel autoclave and heated for 1 h (or $3\ \mathrm{~h~}$ at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and then naturally cooled to room temperature. The $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ decorated carbon $\mathrm{(C)}\overline{{{\cal a}}}\mathrm{Mo}{\cal\mathrm S}_{2},$ ) was collected by centrifugation, washed with distilled water several times, and dried under vacuum condition at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
Synthesis of $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{-C}\textcircled{a}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{0}\mathbf{S}_{2}$ and S-C Composites. The as-prepared $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ (or C) and sulfur powder in a mass ratio of 2:8 were homogenized by grinding, and then sealed in a quartz glass tube under nitrogen atmosphere. The mixture was then heated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ to uniformly distribute sulfur molecules in $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ (or C).
Computational Details. Density functional theory (DFT) was applied for first-principles computations, which were performed by using the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP) with Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) parametrization of the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) as exchange-correlation functional and the projector-augmented wave (PAW) as pseudopotentials. Valence electrons were described by a plane-wave basis with a cut-off energy of $500\mathrm{eV}$ . Periodic slabs were separated along the $z$ direction by a vacuum gap of $15\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The energy convergence criteria were set in the order of $10^{-5}~\mathrm{eV}$ and the structures were relaxed until the force on each atom was less than $0.05\mathrm{eV}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{\-1}$ . The binding energy $E_{\mathrm{{b}}}$ of $\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ or ${\bf S}_{8}$ species with ${\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ was calculated as:
$$
E_{b}=E_{S_{8},L i_{2}S_{n}}+E_{M o S_{2}}-E_{S_{8},L i_{2}S_{n}+M o S_{2}}
$$
where $E_{S_{8},L i_{2}S_{n}+M o S_{2}}$ , $E_{M o S_{2}}$ , and $E_{S_{8},L i_{2}S_{n}}$ represent the total energy of $\mathrm{S}_{8}$ or $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $(\mathtt{n}=1,2$ ,
4, 6, and 8) cluster with $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ , total energy of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ , and total energy of $\mathrm{S}_{8}$ or $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $(\mathtt{n}=1,2$ , 4, 6, and 8) cluster, respectively. The $E_{\mathrm{{b}}}$ values with $\mathrm{C}$ substrate were calculated based on the similar principle.
Visualized Adsorption Test. $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ solution was prepared by dissolving the mixture of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and sulfur with a molar ratio of 1:7 into TEGDME, and then stirring for $24.0\mathrm{h}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an Ar-filled glove box. The concentration of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ solution was set as $2.5~\mathrm{mM}$ . The as-prepared C and $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ with the same weight $\left\langle10\mathrm{mg}\right\rangle$ were dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight, and then added into 5mL $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ solution respectively. For sufficient adsorption, the above mixed solutions were vigorously stirred for $12.0\mathrm{h}$ , and then stayed until complete stabilization for UV-visible absorption test.
Preparation of $\mathbf{Li}_{2}\mathbf{S}_{4}$ Powder and $\mathbf{C}(\pmb{\mathcal{Q}}\mathbf{M0}\mathbf{S}_{2}\mathbf{-Li}_{2}\mathbf{S}_{4}$ for XPS Analysis. Firstly, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ solution was prepared by dissolving stoichiometric amounts of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and sulfur (1:3) in DME/DOL mixed solution $(1{:}1\ \mathrm{v/v})$ . Pure $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ powder was obtained by drying solvent in $0.25\mathrm{~M~Li_{2}S_{4}}$ solution under vacuum condition at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. Dried $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ was dispersed in $0.25{\mathrm{M}}{}$ $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ in DOL/DME (1:1, v/v). After rest for one night, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ adsorbed $\mathrm{C@MoS}_{2}$ (C@MoS2-Li2S4) was obtained by separating solvent.
Nucleation of $\mathbf{Li}_{2}\mathbf{S}$ on C and $\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{M}\mathbf{o}\mathbf{S}_{2}$ . In this case, the concentration of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ solution was set as $0.25~\mathrm{M}$ . To assemble 2025-type coin cells for Li2S nucleation, the modified carbon paper with $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ (or C) was used as working electrode, 0.25 M $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}/$ TEGDME solution with extra $1.0\mathrm{M}$ LiTFSI was used as catholyte, lithium metal was used as counter electrode, and Celgard 3501 was used as separator. Then, $20~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}~\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ catholyte was dropped onto the working electrode, while $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ blank electrolyte without $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ but with 2 wt $\%\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ was added on the counter electrode. The cells were discharged galvanostatically to $2.06\mathrm{V}$ at 0.112 mA and then kept potentiostatically at $2.05\mathrm{V}$ for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ nucleation and growth.
Assembly and Electrochemistry Testing of Symmetric Cell. Firstly, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ solution was prepared by dissolving stoichiometric amounts of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and sulfur (1:5) in DME/DOL mixture solution $(1{:}1\ \mathrm{\v/v})$ . To assemble symmetrical cells, 1.0 M LiTFSI with 0.1 M $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ in DOL/DME (1:1, v/v) was used as electrolyte, and the mixture of $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ (or C) and poly (vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF) with a weight ratio of 9:1 as both the working electrode and counter electrode. The loading mass of $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ (or C) in the electrode is about $1~\mathrm{mg~cm^{-2}}$ . The voltage range for cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurement of symmetric cells was -0.7-0.7 V, and the scan rate ranges from 5 to $50\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ .
Shuttle Current Measurement. The Li-S cells without $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ additive were pre-cycled for three cycles at $0.5~\mathrm{C}$ to stabilize the SEI, and then discharged to $2.38{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and switched to potentiostatic mode to measure the shuttle current. After the potentiostatic mode for 30000 seconds, the cell with S-C or ${\mathrm{S}}–{\mathrm{C}}@{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ electrode was disassembled and digital photographs of lithium metal anode and separator were taken.
Material Characterization. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were performed with FEI Magellan 400 equipped with energy dispersive X-ray spectrometer (EDS). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern were obtained from JEM-1400 microscope. Raman spectroscopy was taken on a Laser Micro-Raman Spectrometer (Invia reflex $\leq10.2\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ) in the wavenumber range of $200{-}2000~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ with a He-Ne laser at $785~\mathrm{nm}$ . Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on ESCAlab250 using Cu-Kα radiation. Nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms were obtained from a Quadrasorb SI gas sorption analyzer. Specific surface area was determined by the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method. Pore-size distribution was analyzed by DFT method. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed on a NETZSCH STA 449C thermal analyzer under nitrogen atmosphere. XPS were collected using an ESCAlab-250 XPS spectrometer equipped with an Al Kα source (1486.6 eV). The UV-visible absorption spectra was obtained from Shimadzu UV-1800. For the ex situ SEM tests of cycled electrodes from Li-S cell, the charged Li-S cell was disassembled after 100 cycles at $2\textrm{C}$ , and then both the cathode and anode were simply washed with DME for following SEM tests. The electron conductivity values of $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ pellets as a function of pressure were measured by four-point probe method.
Electrochemical Measurement. To preparing the cathode, a mixture of ${\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{-C}}\ @{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ (or S-C, $\mathbf{S-MoS}_{2}$ , or $\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ without sulfur loading), Super P carbon and PVDF binder in a weight ratio of 7:2:1 in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP) was casted onto carbon paper and dried at
$50~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ overnight in a vacuum oven to remove solvent. The loading mass of sulfur in electrode is about $1.3~\mathrm{mg~cm^{-2}}$ if not specified. 2025-type coin cells with stainless steel as current collector were assembled inside an Ar-filled glove box ( ${\mathrm{\check{\co}}}.1$ ppm for water and oxygen), and Celgard 3501 was used as separator and lithium metal as anode. The electrolyte was $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ LiTFSI dissolved in DOL/DME (1:1, v/v) with $0.2\mathrm{~M~LiNO}_{3}$ electrolyte additive. During electrochemical testing, both the rate and specific capacity were estimated based on the mass of sulfur in cathode $\mathrm{~(1~C=1672~mA~h~g^{-1}})$ . The galvanostatic measurement of Li-S cells was performed under a mode of cut-off voltage window of 1.8-2.7 V for low rates (0.1 C, 0.2 C, 0.5 C, 1.0 C), of $1.7–2.7\mathrm{~V~}$ for $5.0\mathrm{C}$ and of $1.6{-}2.7\mathrm{~V~}$ for high rates ( $10.0\mathrm{C}$ and $20.0\mathrm{C},$ at room temperature on a LAND-CT2001A battery system. CV measurements and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) testing of Li-S cells were performed using a PARSTAT multichannel electrochemical workstation (Princeton Applied Research, USA). The scan rate and voltage range of CV measurement were $0.1~\mathrm{\mV~\s^{-1}}$ and 1.8-2.7 V, respectively. For the EIS testing, the frequency range was from $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ to $10~\mathrm{mHz}$ . All the electrochemical tests were performed at ambient temperature.
